[
  "ACT 1: COLD OPEN & PERSONAL GROUNDING [00:00 - 03:00]\nScene: 01. Starting Soon -> 02. Monologue / Full Camera\n\nWelcome to The Sound Above. I am Mike Hall, and this is Episode 3. In our last episode, Ray Hightower told me over lunch at Midway Restaurant that I needed to drive up to Libertyville on a Friday and visit a group of developers at 8th Light. Today we revisit my SCNA conversations with 8th Light co-founder Micah Martin and craftsman Mike Jansen on Pair Friday, 8th Light University, and the apprenticeship model that helped spark the Software Craftsmanship movement.\n",
  "ACT 2: SETTING THE HISTORICAL SCENE [03:00 - 08:00]\nScene: 02. Monologue / Full Camera\n\nI still remember the cultural shock of walking up the stairs into 8th Light's second-floor office in downtown Libertyville in the winter of 2008. Coming from enterprise Windows shops with heavy Visual Studio builds and isolated cubicles, that room looked like another world. Long tables were set up with dual keyboards and monitors. Developers like Micah Martin, Eric Smith, Eric Meyer, Colin Jones, and Jim Suchy were pairing in Vim and tmux, running RSpec suites that finished in milliseconds, while MelodySheep's Symphony of Science Carl Sagan remixes played across the room. Anyone from the community could walk in on a Friday, sit down at a keyboard, and write test-driven code side by side with a working craftsman.\n",
  "ACT 3: THE CORE INQUIRY: THE SOUND ABOVE [08:00 - 14:00]\nScene: 02. Monologue / Full Camera -> 04. Split Screen\n\nHere is our core inquiry for Episode 3: How is real engineering judgment transmitted from one practitioner to another? You cannot learn software design purely from a textbook or a vendor certification. At 8th Light, apprenticeship meant sitting next to someone who held the code to a rigorous standard, writing the failing test first, keeping the feedback loop under a second, and explaining the trade-offs out loud at 8th Light University.\n",
  "ACT 4: HALLWAY REWATCH & VIDEO REACTION [14:00 - 28:00]\nScene: 03. Reaction - Video Focus\n\nLet us roll the archive conversations with Micah Martin and Mike Jansen (YouTube ID: GwYGODSJWgM). Listen to how they describe the origins of their apprenticeship program, the transition from Libertyville into downtown Chicago, and why opening their doors to the community every week was central to how they built their engineering culture.\n",
  "ACT 5: THE AI-ERA PARALLEL & TERMINAL WORKBENCH [28:00 - 40:00]\nScene: 05. Workbench - Code & Terminal\n\nThink about what Pair Friday teaches us about working with AI coding agents in 2026. Pairing with an LLM agent requires the exact same discipline as pairing with an eager apprentice at those Libertyville tables. If you let an apprentice write five hundred lines of untested code without a clear boundary, you get a tangle you cannot maintain. If you work in small, verifiable steps, keep your terminal feedback loop fast, and enforce clean interfaces at every seam, both human and machine stay aligned with the architecture.\n",
  "ACT 6: COMMUNITY CODA, OUTRO & NEXT STREAM [40:00 - 45:00]\nScene: 08. Outro & Next Stream\n\nThank you for joining Episode 3 of The Sound Above. Full transcripts, chapter markers, and links to our Libertyville timeline notes are available at just3ws.com. To support our independent compute and archive preservation, visit patreon.com/whoistechcmty. In Episode 4, we sit down with Dave Hoover of Obtiva to explore Geekfest and the psychology behind Apprenticeship Patterns. Until next time: keep your feedback loops fast, and your code humane.\n"
]